## Additional permission to combine with unrar-rs

As an additional permission under section 7 of GNU GPL version 3, the
copyright holders of rarpar grant permission to link or combine covered
rarpar code with the unrar-rs library, including modified versions of that
library governed by the unRAR license restriction, and to convey the
resulting combined work.

This permission does not relicense unrar-rs or any UnRAR-derived material.
Those portions remain governed by their applicable license terms. Nothing in
this permission excuses a distributor from complying with those terms,
including preservation of required license notices. The applicable unRAR
notice is reproduced below and in the unrar-rs license.

The Corresponding Source for a non-source distribution of such a combined
work shall include the exact unrar-rs source used to build it, including any
modifications and the scripts needed to control compilation and installation.

You may remove this additional permission from a modified version of rarpar.
Conveying that modified version combined with unrar-rs requires permission
equivalent to this exception.

## What distributed rarpar contains

`unrar-rs` is a default dependency of this crate: it is pulled in by the
`runtime` feature, and `default = ["runtime", "musl-allocator"]`. An ordinary
`cargo build`, `cargo install` or release archive therefore contains
UnRAR-derived RAR extraction and recovery code, and the unRAR license
restriction travels with that component.

Only a deliberate `--no-default-features` build that also omits `runtime`
links no `unrar-rs`. That configuration is GPL-3.0-or-later alone.

Binary release archives include:

- `LICENSE`: this rarpar license notice.
- `LICENSE.GPL-3.0-or-later`: the full GNU GPL version 3 text.
- `LICENSE.unrar-rs`: the complete unrar-rs license text, including
  the unRAR license restriction.

Source checkouts include the full GNU GPL version 3 text in the workspace root
`LICENSE` file and the complete unrar-rs license in
`crates/unrar-rs/LICENSE`.

## unRAR license restriction

The RAR engine in unrar-rs was developed using the source code of the unRAR
program. All copyrights to the original unRAR code are owned by Alexander
Roshal. The license for the original unRAR code has the following
restriction, which continues to govern the UnRAR-derived code incorporated
into distributed rarpar builds:

UnRAR source code may be used in any software to handle
RAR archives without limitations free of charge, but cannot be
used to develop RAR (WinRAR) compatible archiver and to
re-create RAR compression algorithm, which is proprietary.
Distribution of modified UnRAR source code in separate form
or as a part of other software is permitted, provided that
full text of this paragraph, starting from "UnRAR source code"
words, is included in license, or in documentation if license
is not available, and in source code comments of resulting package.
